A||||28||SCCA FIERO||HELP   please
Z||000000||SCCA FIERO||07-02-99||05:27 PM||||I am trying to get a wiring harness out of my 86 and into my 88.<p>There are 2 huge connections that go through the firewall. One goes to the engine junction box behind the battery. Can those conections be seperated where they go through the firewall? Do I need the one from my V6 car? The V6 car has more wires running under the rear clip (where do they go?) I need to get the wiring harness out of the car and the only way to get it out is to disconnect that connection. The harness is "trapped" between the support for the steering wheel and the heater casing. The only way it will come out without cutting it in half is to get the connection undone.<p>I am doing this as we speak so the sooner the better. Thanks....
Z||000001||SCCA FIERO||07-02-99||05:35 PM||||After looking at it again,I don't think the connection will fit through the space. I think I am gonna have to remove the heater case. Now all I gotta do is figure out is how to remove that huge thing. <br>
Z||000002||Cooter||07-02-99||10:15 PM||||I am not sure if I know which plug you are talking about seperating. If it is the one beside the battery, yes it is in two sections, one goes to the car, the other to the tail lights. The harnes the goes to the tail lights will have more wires in the v6 because of the cooling fan that is located in the right quarter. I believe that you will have to take the rear clip loose to get the rear harness out and swapped. When I put a GT fastback clip on my 4 cyl se, I found out about how different the wiring systems are on the two cars. Good luck in your project, I hope it is worth the work.
Z||000003||SCCA FIERO||07-02-99||10:39 PM||||Thanks for the input Cooter.<p>I had a hell of a time getting the main harness out. I had to undo the junction box in the rear next to the battery and pull it through the firewall and then under the brace for the dash. What a bugger. My finger tips are killing me from undoing all those plugs in both cars. I have the throttle cable from the donor car and in the project car. Tomorrow I will finish installing the main harness from the donor into the project car. Hopefully I will also have the V6 and the 4 banger all disconnected and ready to come out.<p>The "cooling fan" you are talking about is where? I have heard of this but have never seen it. I guess that has to be swapped too.<p>
Z||000004||Cliff Pennock||07-03-99||12:19 AM||||SCCA, I was just wondering: are you making pictures of this swap? It would be interesting to see.
Z||000005||theogre||07-03-99||12:30 AM||||I've never seen one, but I think the v6 aux fan is under the left side grate. it forces air thru the engine compartment. It wasn't used on very many cars as far as I can tell. I've seen a bunch of v6, and haven't seen one yet.
Z||000006||lowCG||07-03-99||01:25 AM||||I laughed the first time I saw those tubes with air blowing out onto the coil,alternator,and whatever else.Was wondering if maybe there was one for my head,inside the car also.<br>First thing I could think of was how much does that #%+@ weigh,and, is it really needed?
Z||000007||SCCA FIERO||07-03-99||10:06 AM||||Pictures? I don't think it would show up,not the difference in harnesses. I did take pictures of the 2 cars side by side before the 86 is gone forever. I will document from here on out and post everything I think is important to know in case anyone is thinking of doing this swap.<p>In the rear of my V6 Fiero there are some silly looking tubes coming out of part between the trunk and engine compartment. Is this where the fan is? To me it looks like the wind is funneled through these tubes and onto the alternator and coil.
Z||000008||Phil||07-03-99||12:59 PM||||I have an 85 GT with the fan. It is located in the rear trunk behind the liner on the pass. side. If it is still working it comes on with the rad fan
Z||000009||Cooter||07-03-99||04:23 PM||||Phil- good answer. The cooling fan is bolted in behind the carpet on the right side of the trunk. It has a relay with it and a ground screw under the right quarter panel. I still dont know if you can get the cooling fan/tail light harness out without taking off the rear clip. DO NOT attempt to use the 4cyl tail light harness and the v6 harness together. They will plug together but are very different. (because of the fan etc. . . )
Z||000010||SCCA FIERO||07-03-99||04:49 PM||||I started to strip my 86 and under the trunk carpet is that fan. I don't know if it even works. Should I install it? If I just use the 4 cylinder harness without the fan will that work?
Z||000011||Cooter||07-03-99||07:39 PM||||SCCA,<br>When I plugged the 6 cyl tail light harness in on my 4 cyl car the tail lights/turn signals did not work and the cooling fan ran at a very slow speed (possibly a ground loop). Oh crap! I just remembered something, the difference in wiring on my cars could have been the fact that I am using a fastback clip on a coupe. The turn signals and brake light wires are very different between the two. I dont know what to tell you, but if you have the time, swap the entire wiring system because I'd hate for you to get everything back together and something not work.
Z||000012||SCCA FIERO||07-03-99||11:20 PM||||The 86 dash and gauges are in the 88. The 86 cradle is out and the V6 is seperated from the 4 speed. To get the V6 and 4 speed apart was a pain. The exhaust pipes before the "Y" were keeping the V6 from pulling away from it. There is a big flange on the 4speed that went up behind the pipe and the clutch wouldn't let it lift enough to clear the flange. I had to take a few parts off to get it free and when I did most of the exhaust bolts that bolt the various pipes together broke. <p>The cradle from the 86 had a bolt that didn't want to come off. On the top and inside the frame is a little bolt type thing that had broke free from the frame and was spinning with the bolt. After 4 Saw-Zall blades,from trying to cut through the bushing and bolt,and 2 hours later I finally cut a hole in the frame to see what was wrong. Finally it was out.<p>I am beat down dead tired....
Z||000013||SCCA FIERO||07-05-99||10:59 PM||||Today I dropped the 88 cradle. Again there was a bolt that didnt want to come out! This time it was one of the front bolts. It would spin,but I hit it with a sledge hammer and it didnt budge an inch. I messed with it for about an hour and finally decided to cut it. I cut it on both sides of the bracket. The cradle was out,but the bolt was still in it. The motor was pulled out of the cradle and I began to drill out the bolt that refuses to come out.<p>The 4 cylinder exhaust was removed and the V6 exhaust was put into place. Those damn springs are very hard to get on and there are only about 20 of them. I have alot of skin missing thanks to those stupid things. The exhaust is on and everything is ready to be put back in the 88. There is still a long way to go though.<p>The V6 has a little mount that looks like a shock absorber on the front of the engine. The are 3 bolts to mount this to the cradle. The 88 cradle has none. I will have to drill and tap some. The V6 bolted right to the Isuzu 5speed and the motor mount was in the right place for the V6. <p><p>Here is something I did not know: The 88 cradle has no bushing where it mounts to the frame of the car. It's amazing what you learn when you tear things apart.<p>I have taken very many pictures Cliff   :)
Z||000014||DJRice||07-06-99||08:04 AM||||Speaking of changing wiring harness swapping......<p>I am working on swapping a blown 84 4cyl for a rebuilt 85 4cyl.  85 is when Pontiac moved the wiring harness over to the battery area.  <p>My question is this.  Is that entire main harness that goes from the battery area, through the firewall, past the ECM and shifter and up under the dash actually completely removable?  I cut the 85 harness in half (like a moron) only to find that the 85 harness had 44 wires in it, and the 84 harness only had 34 wires.   <p>It did not appear to be removeable, but I didnt want to dismantle my entire dashboard.<br>I chose instead to go cut every wire I could find.<p>So now I have the engine installed, but it wont start because the fuel pump does not run and the fuel injector does not inject fuel.  I did "hotwire" the fuel pump, but the injector still does not go.  <p>Anyway, if the harness Im talking about is the same one that SCCA is talking about, is it indeed actually one piece?<br>
Z||000015||SCCA FIERO||07-06-99||09:48 AM||||It is one piece. It runs under the dash to the fuse box and to the little box by the pedals to plug into the front compartment and all the way back to the junction box by the battery. Mine does anyway in my 88 and my 86. To get this thing out you have to remove the dash and pull it from the back by the junction box all the through the firewall and dash. It barely fits through the space where the dash support and the console and heater unit meets. 
Z||000016||DJRice||07-06-99||10:31 AM||||Thanks for the tip. I wish I had never taken my wire clippers out of the toolbox.....Oh well, It should only take several hours to repair the damage......
Z||000017||SCCA FIERO||07-07-99||09:17 PM||||Yesterday I got the cradle back in the car. Today I finished hooking everything up. I then decided it was jugdement time. I pushed in the clutch and turned the key. It fired right up then stopped. It does this everytime I start it. The 4 cylinder fuel tank is still in the car along with the pump and filter. I guess I might have to switch tanks or pumps.<p>I have electrical problems too. When I turn the key on the fan comes on. When I step on the brake the fan turns off but the dash lights come on. Wipers,headlights,blinkers,horn,a/c and heater controls and all the lights in the headliner work. I have no idea what to do about that.
Z||000018||Cooter||07-07-99||10:03 PM||||Did you pull the tail light harness from the v6 car and put it in the 88? If not, this is your problem. If you did, there is probably a bad ground somewhere- have fun finding it.<br>If you are using the 4 cyl fuel tank and pump, you are no providing near enough fuel for the v6- remember that the 4cyl uses throttle body FI with low fuel pressure- somewhere around 15 psi and multi point uses a much higher pressure. How did you connect the 4cyl fuel line to the v6 anyway????
Z||000019||SCCA FIERO||07-08-99||12:19 AM||||I found out the I4 only needed 13PSI and the V6 needs about 45PSI. I wonder if the V6 pump will fit in the 88 tank. I guess I will find out tomorrow. <p>I did pull the rear harness,but I don't know if that would cause the problems I am having. I guess I will put that in too.<p>The 88 fuel lines were right where they needed to be,thank god. The cooling lines are a different story. I spent a whole day getting that to work and I still haven't got that right,but it will work for now.
Z||000020||SCCA FIERO||07-08-99||08:23 PM||||I got the I4 fuel pump out and the V6 unit in. It runs great,but now a new bigger problem is present. I put it in gear and let out the clutch and the car will not move. It tries to,but it seems like the clutch isn't fully letting go. I used the Isuzu throw-out bearing and the V6 clutch. The clutch was not removed or touched. What could it be? 
Z||000021||lowCG||07-08-99||11:07 PM||||Did you check the free play at the clutch arm?<br>If your clutch is still slipping or whatever it's doing,with the clutch slave cylinder removed,you'll have to separate the package again.<br>Can you start it in gear,with the slave cyl. removed,and see if it hooks up?
Z||000022||SCCA FIERO||07-09-99||04:40 PM||||Is it possible that the Isuzu throwout bearing just won't work with the V6 clutch?<p>I removed the slave and that isn't stopping the arm from moving all the way back.
Z||000023||lowCG||07-09-99||05:00 PM||||I know of an unfortunate experience where the mechanic had to put three different clutches in a V6 Fiero because the parts store kept giving incorrect parts and the cores had gone away,this tells me that there are a few different possible combinations.<br>Maybe someone at Archies,V8 shop might know exactly what the situation is,and offer a part number or something.
Z||000024||PaUL||07-10-99||07:50 AM||||SCCAfiero, if you didnt get my email, I think your problem is with the clutch arm. I forgot to mention I had to cut mine down slightly to clear the V6 exhaust. I did this 10 yrs ago. Yours is probably caught under the crossover pipe.  
Z||000025||SCCA FIERO||07-11-99||06:06 PM||||I discovered that the clutch I used was the wrong one. I used the clutch from the V6,I was supposed to use the clutch for the tranny. This kinda makes me feel kinda stupid for doing that,but I build houses not cars. <p>The cradle was dropped once again. This time the correct clutch was installed. I have to get the air out of the clutch then I will know if it works. After 9 days of non-stop working on this thing I have had enough. Today I REST.<p>I still have to install the rear wiring harness and hope that will fix my electrical problems. <br>
Z||000026||SCCA FIERO||07-14-99||07:55 PM||||After dropping the cradle and installing the wrong clutch and dropping it again I am finally driving the beast. This swap wasn't a walk in the park,but I think it's worth it. I had electrical trouble too,but I installed the rear harness from the 86 and EVERYTHING works. 
Z||000027||Cooter||07-14-99||08:54 PM||||Wow- I was right for once. Imagine that! LOL
